.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH rqx-fix 1  "fix " 
.SH NAME
rqx\-fix \- Apply the fixes that have a single correct result, proving each one before it is written. Previews by default
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrqx fix\fR [\fB\-w\fR|\fB\-\-write\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-rounds\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-plan\fR] [\fB\-\-markdown\fR] [\fB\-\-verify\-build\fR] [\fB\-\-reporter\fR] [\fB\-\-level\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-motion\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-\-hyperlinks\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIPATH\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Apply the fixes that have a single correct result, proving each one before it is written. Previews by default
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-write\fR
Write the fixes. Without it no file changes
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-rounds\fR \fI<MAX_ROUNDS>\fR
Maximum number of rounds. One fix can enable another, so fixing repeats until a round finds nothing. Overrides `fix.max_rounds`
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit one JSON object per fixed file instead of the text preview
.TP
\fB\-\-plan\fR
Report the findings `fix` does not change, with the span, the source, the metric and the named refactoring
.TP
\fB\-\-markdown\fR
With \-\-plan, emit Markdown for pasting instead of the terminal report
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\-build\fR
Also run `go vet` on a temporary copy of each Go module with the fixes applied, and withhold a module\*(Aqs fixes if it fails. Needs `go` on PATH
.TP
\fB\-\-reporter\fR \fI<NAME[=PATH]>\fR
Where the output goes: `<name>` for stdout, `<name>=<path>` for a file; repeatable. The names are `terminal` and `json`
.TP
\fB\-\-level\fR \fI<LEVEL>\fR
relaxed, standard, strict or pristine
.TP
\fB\-\-threads\fR \fI<N>\fR
Worker threads. Defaults to the machine\*(Aqs parallelism
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read settings from this file, layered over any `rqx.yaml` found
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR
Do not open the interactive interface; bare `rqx` prints the report instead
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR [default: auto]
When to colour output; `auto` follows NO_COLOR and whether stdout is a terminal
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-motion\fR \fI<MOTION>\fR [default: on]
Animation in the interactive interface
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
on
.IP \(bu 2
off
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Do not draw the progress bar on stderr; `\-\-quiet` implies it
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR \fI<PROGRESS>\fR [default: auto]
How a scan reports progress on stderr; `plain` prints a line every two seconds for a job log
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: A bar on a terminal, nothing in CI or when stderr is redirected
.IP \(bu 2
bar: A bar whenever stderr is a terminal, CI included
.IP \(bu 2
plain: A line on stderr every two seconds and at each phase, for a job log
.IP \(bu 2
none: Nothing
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase output: `\-v` adds the configuration, the cache and every skipped file to the report; `\-vv` also prints stage timings and git commands on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-hyperlinks\fR \fI<HYPERLINKS>\fR [default: auto]
Render `file:line` in the report as a hyperlink that opens in an editor; `auto` enables it on terminals known to support hyperlinks
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: On a terminal known to support them
.IP \(bu 2
always: Whenever the report is coloured
.IP \(bu 2
never: Never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fIPATH\fR] [default: .]

.SH EXTRA
Examples:
  rqx fix                             show what would change
  rqx fix \-w                          write the fixes
  rqx fix \-\-plan                      list the edits instead of a diff
